        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Spalling is a water problem, so control the water.</strong> A penetrating silane or siloxane sealer applied in late summer is the single most effective thing you can do to an existing slab, and it needs redoing every three to five years.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then stop feeding the mechanism: use sand for traction where you can, use the gentler melters sparingly where you must, never use fertiliser as a deicer, and keep salt entirely off concrete in its first winter.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Fix the puddles, extend the downspouts, seal the joints and cracks, and clear snow instead of melting it in place. And if you are pouring new exterior concrete in a climate that freezes, specify an air-entrained mix — it is the one decision that cannot be revisited later.
          </p>
